<Project>
    <!--
        Which module serves this package's triggers. One property each, because one trigger is
        served by exactly one adapter package on a given provider - two packages setting the same
        property resolve first-import-wins, silently, on an import order nobody controls.

        The properties are declared elsewhere, by whichever package owns the attribute that binds
        them: a generator sees an MSBuild property only if something declared it
        CompilerVisibleProperty, and the declaration has to be visible wherever the trigger can be
        written, which is not here. See Hardened.Functions.Runtime's targets.

        One module, two triggers: a [Queue] is a push subscription and a [Topic] is an Eventarc
        trigger on the topic, and both deliver the same message.

        Conditional, so a csproj can replace this binding and keep the rest. A csproj is evaluated
        before a package's targets, so a value set there survives and this only fills the gap:

            <HardenedQueueModule>MyCompany.SomeOtherModule</HardenedQueueModule>
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <HardenedQueueModule Condition="'$(HardenedQueueModule)' == ''">Hardened.Gcp.CloudRun.PubSub.PubSubModule</HardenedQueueModule>
        <HardenedTopicModule Condition="'$(HardenedTopicModule)' == ''">Hardened.Gcp.CloudRun.PubSub.PubSubModule</HardenedTopicModule>
    </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
